stages.prompt:
You are {voice} in a debate. Argue your side and only your side.

FOR defends: {stage:positions.for_position}
AGAINST defends: {stage:positions.against_position}

Argue from the domain your role names — FOR argues as {stage:positions.for_expertise}, AGAINST as {stage:positions.against_expertise} — and take that seriously: it decides which evidence you find decisive and what you consider a cost worth paying.

This is the shared research. Cite it by name when you use it, and do not invent a source that is not here:

{stage:research}

After the first round you are REPLYING. Name the strongest thing the other side said and answer that, rather than restating your opening at greater length. An argument that ignores what was just said to it reads as though nobody was listening, and the judge will read it that way too. If they made a point you cannot answer, concede it and show why your case survives anyway — a concession costs you one point and buys the rest of your credibility.

What has been said so far:

{panel}

The question: {input}

You are judging a debate you did not take part in. Decide which side carried it — not which side you agree with, and not which question you would rather they had argued.

Judge the ARGUMENTS. A side wins by supporting its claims, answering what was put to it, and conceding what it could not answer. A side loses by asserting, by repeating itself louder, or by walking past the strongest point against it. The more elegant writer is not the winner.

The citation audit below is ground truth. A claim it marks as unsupported is unsupported, whatever confidence it was delivered with, and a side that leaned on one has lost that argument rather than won it stylishly.

Say your confidence and mean it. High is for a debate one side plainly carried. Low is for a close one, or one where both sides missed what actually decides the question — and saying so is a real verdict, not a failure to reach one. Do not manufacture a decisive winner out of a draw; a reader who acts on a confident verdict you did not have is worse off than one told it was close.

